Telegram Bot with Python

📌 Introduction

Telegram is a cloud-based messaging application that allows users to send messages, share files, and communicate with others.

A Telegram Bot is an automated program that interacts with users through Telegram. Bots can send messages, respond to commands, and perform different tasks automatically.

🎯 Project Objectives

Learn how Telegram bots work.

Create a Telegram bot using Python.

Handle user messages and commands.

Send automated responses to users.

Understand how to connect Python applications to Telegram.

🛠️ Technologies Used

Python: The main programming language.

Telegram Bot API: For interacting with Telegram.

python-telegram-bot: A Python library for developing Telegram bots.

✨ Features

Respond to user messages.

Handle commands such as /start and /help.

Send automated messages.

Interact with users through a chat interface.

Automate simple tasks.

📦 Installation

Install the required Python library:

pip install python-telegram-bot 

🤖 Creating a Telegram Bot

Open Telegram.

Search for the official @BotFather account.

Send the /newbot command.

Choose a name and username for your bot.

Copy the bot token and keep it private.

💻 Example Code

The following example creates a simple bot that responds to the /start command.

from telegram import Update from telegram.ext import ( Application, CommandHandler, ContextTypes ) TOKEN = "YOUR_BOT_TOKEN" async def start( update: Update, context: ContextTypes.DEFAULT_TYPE ): await update.message.reply_text( "Hello! Welcome to my Telegram bot." ) app = Application.builder().token(TOKEN).build() app.add_handler(CommandHandler("start", start)) app.run_polling() 

Replace YOUR_BOT_TOKEN with your bot token.

🚀 How to Run

Install Python.

Install the required library.

Create a Python file named bot.py.

Add your bot token.

Run the following command:

python bot.py 

Open your bot in Telegram and send /start.

📚 Learning Outcomes

By developing a Telegram bot, I practiced:

Python programming

Working with external APIs

Handling commands and messages

Using Python libraries

Asynchronous programming

Automating tasks with chatbots

🔒 Security

Never share your bot token publicly.

Do not upload tokens or passwords to GitHub.

Use environment variables to store sensitive information.

Add files containing secrets to .gitignore.

